.TH CTI-APPSCAN 1 "2026-10-12" "cti-agent 1.3" "CTI Fleet Manual"
.SH NAME
cti-appscan \- weekly application-security report from the web-application scan notifications in the CTI mailbox
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cti-appscan
.RB [ \-\-since
.IR DURATION ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-detail ]
.RB [ \-\-out
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-text\-out
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-auth\-fail\-out
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-seen\-out
.IR FILE ]
.PP
.B cti-appscan
.BI \-\-commit\-seen " FILE"
.PP
.B cti-appscan
.RB [ \-\-since
.IR DURATION ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-detail ]
.B \-\-subject\-only
.PP
.B sudo cti\-agent cti\-appscan
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cti-appscan
reads the Qualys WAS scan-completion notifications that arrived in the CTI
mailbox and builds the weekly AppSec report for the application-development
audience. It is normally run by
.BR run\-appscan (1)
from
.B cti\-agent\-appscan.timer
every Friday at 07:30, and the report is mailed by
.BR cti\-mailer (1)
.BR "\-\-lane was" ,
which resolves
.B WAS_TO
against
.B WAS_ALLOW_TO
and refuses if either is unset.
.PP
One run:
.TP
.B Reads the notifications
Every message in
.B GRAPH_FOLDER
of
.B GRAPH_MAILBOX
received within
.BR \-\-since .
Mail the fleet sent itself is skipped. Each message is offered to the known
scanners (Qualys WAS only, today); one that is recognised is parsed for the
scan's counts and authentication status. Notifications are deduplicated by
scan reference; one with no reference is kept and logged as not
deduplicated. Parse problems are logged as warnings.
.TP
.B Reconciles with the scan list
Unless
.B \-\-no\-detail
is given and when Qualys credentials are set, the WAS scan list for the
window is fetched and joined to the notifications. This supplies the real
application name and id, the portal link, and scans the scanner ran but
sent no email for (listed without counts). If the list cannot be read, the
report is built from the notifications alone and a warning says so.
.TP
.B Keeps the newest scan per application
Keyed by the scanner's application id, or by the whitespace-collapsed,
lower-cased name when there is no id.
.TP
.B Adds per-finding detail
Again unless
.BR \-\-no\-detail ,
the WAS Findings API is asked for the detail behind each notified scan's
counts. Any failure is a warning and costs only that application's detail.
.TP
.B Writes the report
HTML and plain text, both mode 0600: they say which applications have open
Urgent findings and which are scanned without authentication. Links point
only at known Qualys portal hosts; anything else is rendered as plain text.
.PP
A scan whose configured credential failed to authenticate is logged as
.B AUTH FAILED
and, with
.BR \-\-auth\-fail\-out ,
written to a file for
.B run\-appscan
to pass to
.BR cti\-alert (1).
That is the fleet operator's problem rather than the report audience's, and
this command does not send mail.
.PP
.B cti-appscan
sends nothing, changes nothing in the mailbox, and only reads from Qualys.
.SH OPTIONS
Go's flag syntax: each option may also be written with a single dash
.RB ( \-out ),
and a value may follow
.B =
or a space.
.TP
.BI \-\-since " DURATION"
How far back to read notifications, as a Go duration
.RB ( 168h ,
.BR 336h ).
Also the start of the scan-list and findings window. Not
.BR GRAPH_LOOKBACK_HOURS .
Default:\~\fB168h0m0s\fR (seven days).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-detail
Do not call the Qualys WAS API at all: no scan list, no findings. The
report carries the counts from the notifications only.
Default:\~off.
.TP
.BI \-\-out " FILE"
Write the HTML report to
.IR FILE ,
mode 0600.
Default:\~empty.
.TP
.BI \-\-text\-out " FILE"
Write the plain-text report to
.IR FILE ,
mode 0600. If neither
.B \-\-out
nor
.B \-\-text\-out
is given, the text report goes to standard output.
Default:\~empty.
.TP
.B \-\-subject\-only
Print the subject line on standard output and exit. The mailbox is read,
and the API called, exactly as for a full run; nothing is written, including
the
.B \-\-auth\-fail\-out
file.
Default:\~off.
.TP
.BI \-\-subject\-out " FILE"
Also write the subject line to
.IR FILE ,
mode 0600, during a full run.
.B run\-appscan
uses this rather than a second
.B \-\-subject\-only
run, which would repeat every mailbox and Qualys WAS call.
Default:\~empty (not written).
.TP
.BI \-\-auth\-fail\-out " FILE"
Write one tab-separated line per scan whose configured credential failed:
application, authentication record, status. Mode 0600, and written on every
run, empty when nothing failed, so that an empty file ("checked, none
failed") can be told from an absent one ("not checked"). A failure to write
it is a warning, not a failed run.
Default:\~empty (not written).
.TP
.BI \-\-seen\-out " FILE"
Write the scan notifications this report covered (message IDs, subjects,
received times) to
.IR FILE ,
mode 0600, for
.B \-\-commit\-seen
once the report has been sent. Not written with
.BR \-\-subject\-only .
Default:\~empty (not written).
.TP
.BI \-\-commit\-seen " FILE"
Record a
.B \-\-seen\-out
file in
.IR $FLEET_HOME/state/processed\-appscan.json ,
which
.BR cti\-mailbox (1)
reads to archive the notifications this lane owns, then exit. Nothing else
runs and no Graph credentials are needed.
.BR run\-appscan (1)
calls it only after a real send, never on a dry run or a
.B \-\-to\-dev
test. Needs
.BR FLEET_HOME .
Default:\~empty.
.TP
.BI \-\-tickets\-out " FILE"
Write one Jira ticket's input per application, as JSON, mode 0600, for
.BR "cti\-jira \-\-appscan\-tickets" :
every active, confirmed finding of Serious severity or above (not
potential, not ignored, not fixed), grouped by QID with every URL and
parameter it was seen at, and the fix from the Qualys KnowledgeBase -
Diagnosis, Consequence and Solution for the QID. The owner comes from
.BR APPSCAN_OWNERS_FILE .
The findings are every one the scanner still holds open (NEW, ACTIVE,
REOPENED) however long ago last detected - not only this week's
detections, which would read a finding the week's scan did not re-test as
fixed. An application is marked to be left alone that week when its scan
did not complete, failed to authenticate, was a discovery scan, was not
matched to the scanner's scan list (no web-app id), or its open findings
could not be fetched - so its ticket is never told everything was fixed.
The open-findings search asks Qualys for the verbose record, so each finding
also carries how the scanner found it: the request, any payload, the access
path and an excerpt of the response (bounded, cookie lines redacted, request
headers never read). Not written
with
.BR \-\-subject\-only .
Default:\~not written.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-apps
Print a complete
.I app\-owners.txt
to standard output for every web application defined in Qualys WAS, then
exit: one line per application keyed on its web-app id, with the owner
already on file where there is one and
.B OWNER@example.com
where there is not (the parser refuses that placeholder, so an unedited line
means "no owner", never an assignment). Each web app keeps its
.B covers=
entry; a web app whose URL looks like a staging or test host (a hosting
provider's staging domain, or a first label such as
.BR staging ,
.BR dev ,
.BR test ,
.BR uat )
and has none gets a comment asking what production site it stands in for.
Production sites covered only through a stand-in are listed as comments.
Live websites from the domains lane that no web app's URL points at
(matched on host, www. ignored), and no stand-in covers, follow in a NOT SCANNED section as
.BI site: domain
lines - the owner there is who should get a scan set up, and the line goes
away once a web app covers the site. Lines in the current file that match
nothing are listed at the end as comments. Read-only; needs the
Qualys credentials. A weekly run with
.B \-\-tickets\-out
also checks every web app against the file and names the unowned ones in the
report's JIRA TICKETS section, lists the live websites nothing scans in
a NOT SCANNED section, and the production sites covered only by a stand-in
in a COVERED VIA STAGING section - a stand-in tests the code, not
production's own edge, WAF, TLS or headers, so it is never reported as a
scan of production.
Default:\~off.
.TP
.B \-\-ticket\-slot
Leave an invisible slot in the report for
.B \-\-stamp\-tickets
to fill with a JIRA TICKETS section.
.B run\-appscan
passes it. Default:\~off.
.TP
.BI \-\-stamp\-tickets " FILE"
Read a
.B cti\-jira \-\-ref\-out
file and stamp one line per application - filed, updated, unchanged,
nothing left, not updated and why, or (dry run) what would happen - into
the
.B \-\-out
and
.B \-\-text\-out
reports already written, using the
.B \-\-tickets\-out
file named alongside it; then exit. A missing or unreadable
.I FILE
says each status is unavailable. Links are only https Jira browse links.
Default:\~empty.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.I fleet.env
is loaded at start from
.B FLEET_ENV
(else
.IR $FLEET_HOME/fleet.env ,
else
.IR /var/lib/cti\-agent/fleet.env );
variables already in the environment win.
.TP
.BR TENANT_ID ", " CLIENT_ID ", " CLIENT_SECRET ", " GRAPH_MAILBOX
Microsoft Graph, to read the mailbox. Required.
.I fleet.env
section 1.
.TP
.B GRAPH_FOLDER
Folder to read. Default:\~\fBinbox\fR. Section 1.
.TP
.BR QUALYS_BASE_URL ", " QUALYS_USERNAME ", " QUALYS_PASSWORD
The WAS API. Optional here: with
.B QUALYS_BASE_URL
or
.B QUALYS_USERNAME
empty the API is skipped, as with
.BR \-\-no\-detail ,
and a note says so. A
.B QUALYS_BASE_URL
that does not map to a known Qualys portal leaves report links as plain
text. Section 2.
.TP
.B APPSCAN_OWNERS_FILE
The application owners file. Default:\~\fI/etc/cti\-agent/app\-owners.txt\fR.
One line per application:
.IR "application " | " owner email " [ "| Jira component" "] [" "| covers=host[,host...]" "] [" "| watchers=email[,email...]" ],
where the application is the scanner's web-app id
.RB ( id:123456 ,
which survives a rename) or its exact name; # starts a comment. A missing
file, or an application not in it, means its ticket is filed unassigned and
the report says so. Never in the repository.
.IP
.B covers=
marks a web app as a stand-in for production sites - a staging or test
copy scanned instead of the live site, such as a WP Engine
.I *.wpenginepowered.com
install. Hosts are compared without
.BR www. .
Each covered site is reported under COVERED VIA STAGING instead of NOT
SCANNED, and the web app's ticket says it stands in for production and that
the fix goes into the code, plugins or configuration and is then deployed.
A site a web app scans directly is never listed as via staging.
.B covers=
on a line whose owner is still the placeholder is kept, so regenerating the
file with
.B \-\-list\-apps
does not lose it.
.IP
.B watchers=
lists up to 10 emails added as Jira watchers on the application's ticket
(the owner field stays one email, the assignee). Best-effort: one not found
or refused is skipped. Needs "Manage watcher list" on the Jira API account.
Not shown in the report.
.B \-\-list\-apps
keeps it on lines with a real owner; a placeholder-owner line loses it when
regenerated.
.IP
.BI site: domain
lines name who should get a scan set up for a live website nothing scans
yet; see
.BR \-\-list\-apps .
.IP
Example:
.RS
.nf
id:123456              | dev.lead@example.com | Web Portal
id:777777              | dev.lead@example.com | Web | covers=www.example.com
site:brochure.example.net | web.team@example.com
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B APPSCAN_WEBSITES_FILE
The domains lane's live-website list. Default:\~\fI$FLEET_HOME/state/domains\-websites.json\fR,
written by
.BR run\-domains (1).
Missing means the coverage check is skipped and the log says so; older
than 15 days is noted.
.TP
.B FLEET_ORG
Organisation name in the report banner. Default:\~\fBSecurity\fR.
Section 4.
.TP
.BR GRAPH_LOOKBACK_HOURS ", " QUALYS_KB_MAX_AGE_HOURS ", " MAILBOX_LOOKBACK_HOURS ", " MAILBOX_MIN_AGE_HOURS
Not used, but validated by the shared configuration loader: a
non-integer value, or a mailbox window that breaks its rules (see
.BR cti\-agent (1)),
stops the run before anything is read.
.TP
.BR FLEET_ENV ", " FLEET_HOME
Locate
.IR fleet.env .
.PP
.BR WAS_TO " and " WAS_ALLOW_TO
are read by
.BR cti\-mailer (1),
not by this command.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/cti\-agent/fleet.env
Graph and Qualys credentials.
.TP
.I /etc/cti\-agent/app\-owners.txt
Who owns each application; see
.BR APPSCAN_OWNERS_FILE .
.TP
.I /var/lib/cti\-agent/state/appscan\-YYYY\-MM\-DD.{html,txt}
The report files written by
.BR run\-appscan ,
0600.
.TP
.I /var/lib/cti\-agent/state/appscan\-authfail\-YYYY\-MM\-DD.tsv
The authentication-failure file written for
.BR run\-appscan ,
0600. The runner deletes it before each run, so its absence afterwards
means the check did not happen.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The report (or the subject) was produced. A scan list, findings lookup or
authentication-failure file that failed is a warning, not a failure;
authentication failures in the scans themselves do not change the exit
status either.
.TP
.B 1
Configuration missing or invalid (the message names the variables); the
mailbox could not be read; or a report file could not be written.
.TP
.B 2
An unknown option or an unparseable duration.
.B \-\-help
prints the usage and exits 0.
.SH EXAMPLES
Preview the week's report without sending it:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent run\-appscan \-\-dry\-run
.EE
.PP
Counts only, from the notifications, printed to the terminal:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent cti\-appscan \-\-no\-detail
.EE
.PP
Two weeks instead of one:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent cti\-appscan \-\-since 336h \e
    \-\-text\-out /var/lib/cti\-agent/state/appscan\-two\-weeks.txt
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR run\-appscan (1),
.BR cti\-mailer (1),
.BR cti\-alert (1),
.BR cti\-agent (1)
.PP
RUNBOOK.md section 11f; fleet\-kit/README.md, "The weekly AppSec report".
